{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1572442522,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDI3NTU5OTUyMTQ="},"added":["UX"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1572450973,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU0Nzk3ODEzOQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3848#issuecomment-547978139"},"message":"\u003e so I thought to suggest to throw a warning if -O specified together with --archive (which would imply extraction), but I feel that could be a reasonable use case as well.\n\nI think, that use case introduces an ambiguity to `-O` that should be resolved. How about not interpreting `-O` as the download target in case of `--archive`, but the extraction target, since as for \"regular\" downloads, that is where things get stored in the dataset.\nHowever we could have an additional `-T` or sth to specify a temporary download target for archives (that would be deleted afterwards).","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1572459257,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU0ODA0NTE3NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3848#issuecomment-548045174"},"message":"Not sure yet either we should make semantic of `-O` different (by introducing `-T`) or adding `-E` (extraction target) which would be applicable only in case of `--archive` -- might make options `--help` less convoluted, and `-E` then must be a directory (`-T` in your case could still be a directory or a filepath, making it impossible to verify similarly to the case with \"too flexible\" `-O`)","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1572511573,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU0ODI2OTAxNA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3848#issuecomment-548269014"},"message":"That `-E` is also an option, yes.\nI have no strong opinion which of that two approaches would make `-O` more consistent. Depends whether you are aware of what's going on beneath and therefore see at as download target or you look at the dataset and see it as the storage location within that dataset, that is the result of your command call.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1572536405,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU0ODQzNjI1Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3848#issuecomment-548436256"},"message":"I'm confused why the discussion has been focused on `--archive`.  The core issue seems to be that `-O` has special treatment for existing directories and if there's a typo, download-url of course isn't aware of that directory and goes with the non-directory treatment.  The download goes somewhere the user didn't intend; I don't see why conceptually it matters if later that download file is fed to `add_archive_content` or not (though I understand that in practice the resulting explosion of the archive's content in the wrong place may be more annoying).\n\nI think a good option here is to go with rsync-like behavior and make a trailing separator necessary to trigger the directory treatment.  (Yes, I know when similar cases have come up before, some object to this as too magical, but the current behavior is already magical and in a way that is less familiar.)  The trailing slash behavior is easy to document and resolves the ambiguity of guessing what the user wants.  And because we know when they want a directory, we can even create directories that don't exist.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1572537228,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU0ODQ0Mjg1NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3848#issuecomment-548442854"},"message":"But we could have a really complicated solution! ;-)\n\nSomewhat embarrassing, but I think you're absolutely right, @kyleam .","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1572537433,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU0ODQ0NDU0NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3848#issuecomment-548444544"},"message":"I thought to argue with `wget` as an argument (I thought its behavior was the inspiration for the flexibility) but\n```shell\n$\u003e wget -O /tmp/out https://launchpad.net/libeatmydata\n/tmp/out: Is a directory\n```\n\ntrailing slash... I somewhat hate it (since generally we are trying to get away from such semantic), but it would indeed be the best solution here probably... and crash if there is no trailing slash and there is a directory in the path (similar to `wget`).  I would be ok with that breaking change.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1572538434,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU0ODQ1MjA4OQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3848#issuecomment-548452089"},"message":"\u003e I somewhat hate it (since generally we are trying to get away from such semantic)\n\nI'd be interested to read past discussions on this (though of course not something you should waste much time searching for).  Off the top of my head, I can't think of many places in DataLad where we make the distinction.  The main one I know of is `status`, but I didn't think there was any proposal to get away from that behavior.\n\nhttps://github.com/datalad/datalad/blob/afc32703d5cad054c95a249faa9dd2e6c5f41c11/datalad/core/local/status.py#L186-L191","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1572773571,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU0OTExOTQzMA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3848#issuecomment-549119430"},"message":"\u003e I'd be interested to read past discussions on this\n\nI don't have the references either, but some difficulty of working with such semantics comes from the fact that once a path arg is turned into a `Path` object, such properties can no longer be inferred. That being said, it is now relatively clear to me that any kind of argument normalization has to be carefully considered, and actually largely avoided when passing args to underlying (but also high-level) commands. Hence this is not so much of an issue than I thought it would be. And clearly, I considered it meaningful for `status`, so I am on board with this move.","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1575905676,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50Mjg2Njc5Mjg0OQ=="},"status":2}]}